{
  "summary": "Includes the network identifier in `CService::GetKey()` so that addresses on different networks (such as Tor and I2P) cannot produce identical key representations. Retains a legacy network-less key method specifically for `AddrMan` to preserve compatibility with existing `peers.dat` databases.",
  "problem": "Because `CService::GetKey()` omitted the network type, two addresses on different networks (such as Tor and I2P) could produce the same key bytes. In per-peer bloom filters tracking known addresses, this theoretical collision could cause a node to falsely believe a peer already knows an address (such as `foo.onion` after sending `foo.i2p`) and suppress relaying it.",
  "evidence": [
    "PR description: \"CService::GetKey() didn't include the network in the output. So in theory it would be possible for two IP2 and Tor addresses to have the same output... This would have unexpected results when used in the per-peer bloom filter of known addresses - we may wrongfully assume that a peer knows foo.onion when we have sent to them foo.i2p.\""
